{
  "id": "80532",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Cell Potentials at Nonstandard Conditions: The Nernst Equation",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Most of the redox processes that interest science and society do not occur under standard state conditions, and so the potentials of these systems under nonstandard conditions are a property worthy of attention. Having established the relationship between potential and free energy change in this module, the previously discussed relation between free energy change and reaction mixture composition can be used for this purpose."
              }
            ],
            "id": "useyrnx6f1u18cz",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "cd1ligs65tfk58o",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2zm9k58nlwxvmbh",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\text{Δ}G\\;=\\;\\mathrm\\Delta G^\\circ\\;+\\;RT\\;\\ln\\;Q",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Notice the reaction quotient, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": ", appears in this equation, making the free energy change dependent on the composition of the reaction mixture. Substituting the equation relating free energy change to cell potential yields the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Nernst equation"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An equation used to calculate the cell potential of an electrochemical cell under nonstandard conditions, using the standard cell potential, ion concentrations, and temperature."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "135732038",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2222541158",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ":"
              }
            ],
            "id": "dvkaey5z231uxtz",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "e1944vkb4830i2o",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "6zqnifhbpfecjev",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "-nFE_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\;=\\;-nF{E_\\mathrm{cell}^\\circ}_\\;+\\;RT\\;\\ln\\;Q",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "or"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4gzvg2cf04r51rt",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "i2my4q6996womvq",
            "type": "callout"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "mhvsxplkyabuqxc",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "83978cjv26jowmn",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "E_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\;=\\;{E_\\mathrm{cell}^\\circ}\\;-\\;\\frac{RT}{nF}\\;\\ln\\;Q",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "This equation describes how the potential of a redox system (such as a galvanic cell) varies from its standard state value, specifically, showing it to be a function of the number of electrons transferred, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "n"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the temperature, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "T"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and the reaction mixture composition as reflected in "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Substituting in the values of the fundamental constants ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "R"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "F"
              },
              {
                "text": "), T = 298 K, and a factor converting from natural to base-10 logarithms yields a simplified version of the Nernst equation at 25 °C:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3fqwem48zrv1sjo",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "k4afgm3at3i1l1f",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "gj7vvpva22t4vqa",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "E_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\;=\\;{E_\\mathrm{cell}^\\circ}\\;\\;-\\;\\frac{0.0592\\;V}n\\;\\log\\;Q",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "1691283530",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "985988595",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Use the Nernst equation to predict the spontaneity of the redox reaction shown below."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "8eo3gh77a0e9g8n",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "r7866qndnqaoo1w",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "kelo6lpnntx3l7q",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\mathrm{Co}(s)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}(aq,\\;1.94\\;\\mathrm M)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{Co}^{2+}(aq,\\;0.15\\;\\mathrm M)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Fe}(s)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "First we need to determine "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "E"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "°"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "cell"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ":"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "xk0sl63vgcytwgl",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "mr73aegg8gihjg5",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1ebcmhche1q3mvv",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{Anode}\\;(\\mathrm{oxidation}):\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{Co}(s)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{Co}^{2+}(aq)\\;+\\;2\\;\\mathrm e^-\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;E_{\\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\\mathrm{Co}}^\\circ\\;=\\;-0.28\\;\\mathrm V\\\\\\mathrm{Cathode}\\;(\\mathrm{reduction}):\\;\\;\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}(aq)\\;+\\;2\\;\\mathrm e^-\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{Fe}(s)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;E_{\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\\mathrm{Fe}}^\\circ\\;=\\;-0.447\\;\\mathrm V\\end{array}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "qki6wr50zdi0ijs",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "99up78mgrmwfgyr",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "E_{\\mathrm{cell}}^\\circ\\;=\\;E_{\\mathrm{cathode}}^\\circ\\; -\\;E_{\\mathrm{anode}}^\\circ\\;=\\;-0.447\\;\\mathrm V\\;-\\;(-0.28\\;\\mathrm V)\\;=\\;-0.17\\;\\mathrm V",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Notice the negative value of the standard cell potential indicates the process is not spontaneous under standard conditions."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4l0e3oy4k0c1009",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Substitution of the Nernst equation terms for the nonstandard conditions yields:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "vykr1fw6271vazq",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "ochhbs3zqdzbplf",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "vrkvtmba5ja0lre",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\begin{array}{l}Q\\;=\\;\\dfrac{\\lbrack\\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\\rbrack}{\\lbrack\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\\rbrack}\\;=\\dfrac{\\;0.15\\;\\mathrm M}{1.94\\;\\mathrm M}\\;=\\;0.077\\\\E_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\;\\;=\\;\\;{\\mathrm E^\\circ}_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\;-\\;\\;\\dfrac{0.0592\\;\\mathrm V}n\\;\\log\\;Q\\\\E_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\;=\\;-0.17\\;\\mathrm V\\;-\\;\\dfrac{0.0592\\;\\mathrm V}2\\;\\log\\;0.077\\\\E_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\;=\\;-0.17\\;\\mathrm V\\;+\\;0.033\\;\\mathrm V\\;=\\;-0.14\\;\\mathrm V\\end{array}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The cell potential remains negative (slightly) under the specified conditions, and so the reaction remains nonspontaneous."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "9r58g1hdkoibaxc",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1796496327",
            "selection": {
              "anchor": {
                "offset": 0,
                "path": [
                  0,
                  0
                ]
              },
              "focus": {
                "offset": 0,
                "path": [
                  1,
                  0
                ]
              }
            },
            "type": "content"
          }
        ],
        "id": "1878310925",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "example",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The following steps take you through the process of calculating the cell potential for the reaction described in the cell notation below at room temperature."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "fn097vaj6z47xk6",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "x10dr5cpv0ba1x4",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "h1q2zfccwjdi8m8",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\mathrm{Al}(s)│\\mathrm{Al}^{3+}(aq,\\;0.15\\;\\mathrm M)║\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq,\\;0.025\\;\\mathrm M)│\\mathrm{Cu}(s)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Use the "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Standard Reduction Potentials"
                      }
                    ],
                    "href": "/course/link/iz1ln_standard_reduction_potentials_mv70v",
                    "idref": "80874",
                    "target": "new",
                    "type": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " table as needed."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "x9i2z0ao4zyt2gk",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2340253927",
            "selection": {
              "anchor": {
                "offset": 0,
                "path": [
                  0,
                  0
                ]
              },
              "focus": {
